<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward James Roye</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1872">1872</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward James Roye was the fifth president of Liberia, serving from 1870 to 1871. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, he became a wealthy merchant and the first president of full African descent. His government raised a costly loan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on harsh terms, and amid controversy over it and his attempt to extend his term he was deposed in 1871; he died in 1872.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-james-roye.jpg" alt="Edward James Roye" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miscellaneous Items in High Demand, PPOC, Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_James_Roye_c._1850.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Roye was born free on 3 February 1815 in Newark, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, the son of John and Nancy Roye, formerly enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> who had moved there from Kentucky and were reportedly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> origin. His father ran a ferry across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wabash-river">Wabash River</a> at Terre Haute, Indiana, and owned property in three towns, so the son could attend Ohio University. After his father's death in 1836 he moved to Terre Haute and ran the African-American community's largest barbershop.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Drawn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>'s promotion of emigration, Roye moved to Liberia with his family in 1846, reportedly bringing $1,000 of capital. Within three years he was in politics, serving as speaker of the House of Representatives in 1849–1850, as Secretary of the Treasury and later as Chief Justice from 1865 to 1868; he lost the presidential election of 1855. In 1857 he bought the brig Eusebia to trade camwood, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a> and New York City, and by 1870 his fortune was put at $200,000.</p><p>Inaugurated on 3 January 1870, he was the first member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-whig-party">True Whig Party</a> to become president. Liberia's imports cost far more than its exports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> earned. Roye sought to lengthen the presidential term from two years to four; a referendum passed the change, but the Republican Party disputed the result and held an election in May 1871, which Roye boycotted and which its candidate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-jenkins-roberts">Joseph Jenkins Roberts</a> claimed unopposed. The speaker William Spencer Anderson negotiated a loan of $500,000 from British financiers, on terms worse than the legislature had approved.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>News of the loan set off riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, and Roye was removed on 26 October 1871, in what some allies called a coup d'état. Charged with treason, he was convicted on 10 February 1872 and sentenced to hang. He died a few days later in uncertain circumstances: a 1926 history says a mob beat him to death after an escape attempt, while contemporary despatches from the U.S. minister in Liberia say he drowned swimming for an English ship. Royesville is named after him, his portrait appeared on the five-dollar note, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-whig-party">True Whig Party</a>'s headquarters was called the Edward J. Roye Building.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward James Roye” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Newark, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monrovia">Monrovia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/liberia">Liberia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.06,-82.42&amp;year=1852" aria-label="Newark on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2522 731 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Edward James Roye"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2972 1001h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2972 1001h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.06,-82.42&amp;year=1852">What was here in 1852? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
